<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00KJ">Example <span data-tag="00KJ">2.4.2.16</span> <span class="named">(Topologically Enriched Categories)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{Top}$ denote the category of topological spaces. The formation of singular simplicial sets (Construction <a data-tag="001R" href="/tag/001R">1.2.2.2</a>) determines a functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }: \operatorname{Top}\rightarrow \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}\quad \quad X \mapsto \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) \]
</div>
<p> which preserves finite products (in fact, it preserves all small limits), and can therefore be regarded as a monoidal functor from $\operatorname{Top}$ to $\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ (where we endow both $\operatorname{Top}$ and $\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ with the cartesian monoidal structure). Applying Remark <a data-tag="00EH" href="/tag/00EH">2.1.7.4</a>, we see that every topologically enriched category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ can be regarded as a simplicial category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{\bullet }$ having the same objects, with morphism simplicial sets given by </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{\bullet } = \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }( \underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) ); \]
</div>
<p> here $\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ denotes the set of morphisms from $X$ to $Y$, endowed with the topology determined by the topological enrichment of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (see Example <a data-tag="00EL" href="/tag/00EL">2.1.7.8</a>). </p>
</article>
